{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"Given that \\( z \\) is a pure imaginary root of the equation \\( z^2 + z + \\omega = 0 \\) and \\( |z| = 1 \\), we can let \\( z = yi \\) where \\( y \\) is a real number. Since \\( z \\) is pure imaginary, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n(yi)^2 + yi + \\omega = 0\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\\[\n-y^2 + yi + \\omega = 0.\n\\]\nSeparating the real and imaginary parts, we get:\n\\[\n-y^2 + \\omega = 0 \\quad \\text{(real part)} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = 0 \\quad \\text{(imaginary part)}.\n\\]\nThe imaginary part \\( y = 0 \\) would imply that \\( z = 0i = 0 \\), which is not a pure imaginary number. Therefore, we must have:\n\\[\n-y^2 = \\text{Re}(\\omega) \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = -\\text{Im}(\\omega).\n\\]\nSince \\( |z| = 1 \\), we have \\( y^2 = 1 \\), so \\( y = \\pm 1 \\). Therefore, we can consider two cases:\n1. \\( y = 1 \\), so \\( \\omega = 1 - i \\) and \\(\\overline{\\omega} = 1 + i\\).\n2. \\( y = -1 \\), so \\( \\omega = -1 + i \\) and \\(\\overline{\\omega} = -1 - i\\).\n\n","prefix_end":920,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[22043,429,17767,1147,1124,8,374,264,10526,49952,3704,315,279,23606,17767,1147,61,17,488,1147,488,1124,32696,284,220,15,1124,8,323,17767,760,89,91,284,220,16,1124,701,582,646,1077,17767,1147,284,61697,1124,8,1380,17767,379,1124,8,374,264,1931,1372,13,8704,17767,1147,1124,8,374,10526,49952,11,279,23606,9044,510,59,9640,7021,72,29776,17,488,61697,488,1124,32696,284,220,15,198,59,921,8206,15491,9606,311,510,59,9640,12034,61,17,488,61697,488,1124,32696,284,220,15,624,59,921,14492,1095,279,1931,323,49952,5479,11,582,633,510,59,9640,12034,61,17,488,1124,32696,284,220,15,1124,31610,1124,1318,96065,7951,949,9139,1124,31610,1124,1318,90,437,92,1124,31610,379,284,220,15,1124,31610,1124,1318,96065,28016,3287,949,9139,624,59,921,785,49952,949,17767,379,284,220,15,1124,8,1035,33508,429,