6751594543,-0.00017414960893802345,-5.519237674889155e-05,0.0,-1.0609570381348021e-05,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-1.7881377516459906e-06,-7.152555099310121e-07,0.0,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-0.010704596526920795,-0.0002752163854893297,-0.002008565468713641,-0.032468222081661224,-0.34909433126449585,-2.3841855067985307e-07,-0.0004724340105894953,-0.0239129476249218,-0.4123426675796509,-0.00676448829472065,-0.00012206286191940308,0.0,-0.0015948207583278418,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-3.135155202471651e-05,-0.0027567262295633554,-0.0020453264005482197,-0.37449315190315247,-5.960462772236497e-07,-1.2636104656849056e-05,-4.0531076592742465e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,57960,32696,3,387,264,6351,1372,1741,429,400,22957,32696,91,284,220,16,4779,323,279,23606,198,78045,89,61,17,488,1147,488,1124,32696,284,220,15,59,60,4648,264,10526,49952,3704,400,89,2418,220,7379,57960,32696,488,1124,1975,1056,35702,32696,92,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $\\omega$ be a complex number such that $|\\omega| = 1,$ and the equation\n\\[z^2 + z + \\omega = 0\\]has a pure imaginary root $z.$  Find $\\omega + \\overline{\\omega}.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"c83d3dd7a072b28821f82dffb8a2373b7db9dda307b025ed1c97d43a681622c4"},"seconds":16.73544931411743,"seed":393766518889228722,"stop":"eos","text":"Given that \\( z \\) is a pure imaginary root of the equation \\( z^2 + z + \\omega = 0 \\) and \\( |z| = 1 \\), we can let \\( z = yi \\) where \\( y \\) is a real number. Since \\( z \\) is pure imaginary, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n(yi)^2 + yi + \\omega = 0\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\\[\n-y^2 + yi + \\omega = 0.\n\\]\nSeparating the real and imaginary parts, we get:\n\\[\n-y^2 + \\omega = 0 \\quad \\text{(real part)} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = 0 \\quad \\text{(imaginary part)}.\n\\]\nThe imaginary part \\( y = 0 \\) would imply that \\( z = 0i = 0 \\), which is not a pure imaginary number. Therefore, we must have:\n\\[\n-y^2 = \\text{Re}(\\omega) \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = -\\text{Im}(\\omega).\n\\]\nSince \\( |z| = 1 \\), we have \\( y^2 = 1 \\), so \\( y = \\pm 1 \\). Therefore, we can consider two cases:\n1. \\( y = 1 \\), so \\( \\omega = 1 - i \\) and \\(\\overline{\\omega} = 1 + i\\).\n2. \\( y = -1 \\), so \\( \\omega = -1 + i \\) and \\(\\overline{\\omega} = -1 - i\\).\n\nIn both cases, we calculate \\( \\omega + \\overline{\\omega} \\):\n1. For \\( \\omega = 1 - i \\) and \\(\\overline{\\omega} = 1 + i\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\omega + \\overline{\\omega} = (1 - i) + (1 + i) = 2.\n\\]\n2. For \\( \\omega = -1 + i \\) and \\(\\overline{\\omega} = -1 - i\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\omega + \\overline{\\omega} = (-1 + i) + (-1 - i) = -2.\n\\]\n\nHowever, since the problem does not specify which root to use, we need to consider the magnitude condition. The magnitude of \\( \\omega \\) is \\( |\\omega| = 1 \\), and we need to check which of the two values fits. In both cases, the magnitude is \\( \\sqrt{1^2 + 1^2} = \\sqrt{2} \\), which does not satisfy \\( |\\omega| = 1 \\). However, the correct interpretation is that the sum of the real parts of \\( \\omega \\) and its conjugate is always twice the real part of \\( \\omega \\), and the only consistent real part that satisfies \\( |\\omega| = 1 \\) is when \\( \\omega = \\pm i \\), giving \\( \\omega + \\overline{\\omega} = 0 \\).\n\nThus, the correct answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{0}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:694"}
